  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/...\nIssued 311 AM MST TUE NOV 10 2026\nA deep trough will approach the coast on Friday, increasing\nsouthwest flow across the area. It will become windy across\nthe open terrain of southeast Oregon and far southwest Idaho\nwith gusts 35-50 mph, and 20-40 mph elsewhere. Highs on Friday\nwill remain near record highs, warmest across southwest Idaho. \nThe trough will weaken as it moves inland, but will push a cold \nfront across the area late Friday into Saturday with rain and \nhigh mountain snow. Precipitation chances will rise to 50-80% \nFriday night through Saturday morning, highest across the \nmountains. Rainfall amounts will be light in the valleys with \nlight to moderate totals in the mountains. Snow levels will fall\nfrom 7-8kft MSL Friday evening to 6-7kft by Saturday morning, \nkeeping snowfall confined to higher mountains. Winds will remain\ngusty on Saturday, but not as strong as Friday. Highs Saturday \nwill cool 5-10 degrees.\n\nA brief dry but continued gusty period is likely late Saturday \ninto early Sunday as the first system moves east and another \ndeep low approaches the coast. This next system will gradually \nmove inland Sunday and Monday, bringing a 40-60% chance of \nprecipitation to the area throughout the two day period. Chances\nwill likely increase/decrease across narrower periods as \nconfidence in timing and details rises in subsequent forecasts. \nWinds will slacken but continue to be breezy as the trough moves\nin and the surface pressure gradient weakens. Temperatures will\nremain similar on Sunday, then cool several more degrees on \nMonday as colder air arrives with the trough. Snow levels \nshould also decrease to 4-6kft by Monday. Precipitation chances \ntrend lower on Tuesday, but troughing overhead will keep \nconditions unsettled with temperatures cooling a few more \ndegrees.",
